//
// A key-field write through an element view materialises the view — and the container's own
// `?` does not change that.
//
// @PLN130 F4 made this a RESHAPE: the view materialises, the author is told, and the
// collection keeps a consistent reachable entry.  `146-keyed-rekey-through-view` guards it on
// a DENSE container.  One `?` on the container defeated all of it: no advice, the record
// re-keyed in place, and the element left reachable by NEITHER key while iteration still
// yielded it — which is F4's own description of the defect it was written to remove, alive
// again behind a nullability marker (loft#1456).
//
// FIVE sites read the non-null spelling only, and all five are needed — the first two decide
// whether the materialise is ASKED FOR, the last three whether it HAPPENS:
//
//   * `note_key_field_write`'s shape test — a `ref(E)?` view failed `Type::Reference(_, _)`
//     and the function returned before doing anything;
//   * its container match — `Optional(Sorted(..))` fell past every keyed arm to `_ => continue`;
//   * `scopes::nullable_view_locals` — classifies by the defining STATEMENT's shape and not by
//     the deps, so a local `make_independent` had already detached was still marked never-free;
//   * `@FR-O-Proxy`'s materialise gate in the interpreter's generator, and
//   * its NATIVE twin, which asks `heap_def_nr` twice — once to select the arm and once to
//     emit it, so peeling only one leaves the arm chosen and unreachable.
//
// The last two are why this is a both-backends cell rather than an interpreter one: with only
// the interpreter peeled, `--interpret` was correct and `--native` still lost the element, and
// nothing in the corpus would have said so.
//
// The DENSE twin of every cell is here and must stay correct: it is what says the fix is about
// the `?` and not about keyed writes in general, and it is the control that was already
// passing when the nullable one was not.
//
// @falsified-at: 49bd8c4f2 — MEASURED, both backends.  On that build the `nullable_*` cells
//   read `s[30]` and `s[5]` as absent while iteration yields the record, and no advice is
//   emitted; the dense cells pass there unchanged, which is what makes this a guard for the
//   nullability axis rather than for @PLN130 F4 as a whole.
//
// The advice itself is not pinned here, matching `146`'s convention for this family: the
// ASSERTS are what carry the contract, and the advice is the same sentence `146` already
// scores on the dense side.  Pinning it twice would make this file fail on a rewording that
// `146` would also catch.
// @falsified-by: tests/falsified/1456-a-key-write-through-a-nullable-collections-view-materialises.patch
//   — RE-SCORED against this patch, because the control above records its measurement in
//   prose and carries no channel list to match against.  That control is reachable from no
//   public ref, so the patch is the runnable form of this receipt: it reintroduces the fault
//   on top of HEAD.  Score it with `scripts/falsify.sh <this file> --patch <that patch>`.
//   The channels here are this patch's own, and agree with what the prose above describes.

struct Elm1456 { key: integer, tag: integer }

fn dense_keeps_the_element() {
  s: sorted<Elm1456[key]> = [Elm1456 { key: 10, tag: 111 }, Elm1456 { key: 30, tag: 333 }];
  c = s[30];
  c.key = 5;
  assert(c.key == 5, "the view's own copy takes the write: {c.key}");
  assert(c.tag == 333, "and keeps the rest of the record: {c.tag}");
  assert((s[30].tag ?? -1) == 333, "the element stays reachable at its ORIGINAL key: {s[30].tag ?? -1}");
  assert((s[5].tag ?? -1) == -1, "and is NOT reachable at the written one: {s[5].tag ?? -1}");
  n = 0;
  for e in s { n = n + e.key; }
  assert(n == 40, "iteration still yields both records, unrenamed: {n}");
}

fn nullable_keeps_the_element() {
  s: sorted<Elm1456[key]>? = [Elm1456 { key: 10, tag: 111 }, Elm1456 { key: 30, tag: 333 }];
  c = s[30];
  c.key = 5;
  assert(c.key == 5, "the view's own copy takes the write: {c.key}");
  assert(c.tag == 333, "and keeps the rest of the record: {c.tag}");
  assert((s[30].tag ?? -1) == 333, "the element stays reachable at its ORIGINAL key: {s[30].tag ?? -1}");
  assert((s[5].tag ?? -1) == -1, "and is NOT reachable at the written one: {s[5].tag ?? -1}");
  n = 0;
  for e in s? { n = n + e.key; }
  assert(n == 40, "iteration still yields both records, unrenamed: {n}");
}

fn main() {
  dense_keeps_the_element();
  nullable_keeps_the_element();
}
